{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["The reason for <b>\"From these the islands of the nations were separated in their lands\"</b> is that the sons of Japheth are dwellers in the islands of the sea, and they are separated, each one from his brothers, each dwelling alone on a different island, and their lands are distant from one another. And this is the blessing of their father, for he said, \"May Elohim enlarge Japheth\" (Genesis 9:27) — that they would be many in the broad places of the earth. But the sons of Ham all dwell close together in their lands, therefore Scripture says, \"And the border of the Canaanite was from Sidon\" (Genesis 10:19), and it says, \"in their lands, among their nations\" (Genesis 10:20), and likewise regarding the sons of Shem. And Scripture recounts all these because it desired to make known the lineage of Abraham from Shem and the descendants of Ham, to make known the nations whose land Abraham merited because of the sin of their fathers. Therefore it also recounted Japheth and the division, to make known the cause of the change of languages and their dispersion to the ends of the earth within a short time after the first man. Furthermore, to make known the lovingkindness of HaShem and His keeping of the covenant with Noah, for He did not wipe them out. And the Rav said in the Guide of the Perplexed (III:50) that this validates for the hearers the novelty of the world's creation. And this too is true, for Abraham our father commands his children and his household after him, and testifies to them about Noah and his sons, who saw the Flood and were in the ark — and behold, he is a witness from the mouth of a witness concerning the entire matter of the Flood, and a fourth witness to the Creation, for Noah saw his father, who saw the first man, and Isaac and Jacob saw Shem, the witness to the Flood, and Jacob declares all this to those who went down to Egypt, even to Pharaoh and the men of his generation, and the people in every generation know from their fathers — four or five generations reporting their deeds and their origins:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The Rav's citation is to Guide of the Perplexed III:50 (חלק ג פרק נ). Ramban quotes the chapter reference in Hebrew as 'ג נ'; this is resolved as part 3, chapter 50."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:26:58.709860123Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}